+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`src/index.ts` already holds JSON Pointer and JSON Patch code with private helpers: `isObject`, `setMember` (uses `defineProperty` so `__proto__` stays an ordinary member) and `cloneJson`. Callers' inputs are untrusted and must not be mutated.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Implement the RFC 7396 §2 `MergePatch` algorithm exactly.
+- Return a fresh value that shares no structure with the inputs.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- Diffing two documents into a merge patch.
+- Input validation beyond JSON-shaped values. The algorithm is total, so nothing throws.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- The algorithm is recursive. If `patch` is not an object, return `cloneJson(patch)`. Otherwise start from a clone of `target` if it is an object, else `{}`. For each own key of `patch`, a `null` value deletes the key from the result. Any other value is stored through `setMember` as `mergePatch(result[key] if own, patch[key])`.
+- Read target members with `Object.hasOwn`, so inherited properties such as `constructor` are never treated as target members.
+- Clone via `cloneJson`, so results never alias the inputs. `undefined` as a target is treated as a non-object, i.e. `{}`.
+- Deleting uses `delete`, which removes an own `__proto__` safely. Only own members are touched.
+- RFC 7396 obsoletes RFC 7386, which the request names. The algorithm is identical, so the spec cites 7396.
+- RFC 7396 §3 notes that `null` cannot be set as a member value. This is a property of the format, and the standard wins over convenience.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- Recursion depth follows the nesting of the JSON, as with the existing `cloneJson`. Accepted.
+- A `undefined` patch is not valid JSON. It is returned as-is, like any non-object.
